!!! Sessions: server-side state keyed by a cookie.
!!!
!!! READ THIS BEFORE USING IT IN PRODUCTION.
!!!
!!! This module does not generate session identifiers, and that is deliberate.
!!! A session id is a bearer credential: anyone holding one is the user. It
!!! must come from a cryptographically secure source, and there is no such
!!! source reachable from Matrix today:
!!!
!!!   * nil.std.math is MINSTD, a 31-bit linear congruential generator whose
!!!     global stream starts at the seed 12345. Its own documentation says
!!!     "neither API is cryptographic". Two draws reveal the stream.
!!!   * nil.std.security has base64 and hex, and no hash, no HMAC, no random.
!!!   * The runtime has no crypto or random intrinsic of any kind.
!!!   * /dev/urandom cannot be read: file.read_regular_bytes refuses a
!!!     character device by design, and file.read_bytes returns empty for one.
!!!
!!! So `start-session` takes the id from its caller, and the caller is
!!! expected to get it from the same native shim that will be providing the
!!! socket — `getrandom(2)` is one more export on a library that has to exist
!!! anyway. `development-id` below exists so the tests and the demo can run,
!!! is named so it cannot be used by accident, and says what it is.
!!!
!!! Everything else here — expiry, the store, and above all the cookie
!!! attributes — is the part that can be got right today, and is.

book nilweb.session.session

read nil.std.text
read everything from nilweb.text.scan
read everything from nilweb.http.headers
read everything from nilweb.http.request
read everything from nilweb.http.response


public session-value begin record
    field name as text
    field content as text
end record


public session begin record
    field id as text
    field values-held as array of session-value
    !!! Milliseconds since the epoch, as `now in milliseconds` reports them.
    field expires-at as number
end record


public session-store begin record
    field sessions as array of session
    !!! How long a session lives from its last touch, in milliseconds.
    field lifetime as number
end record


!!! --- A store. One hour is the default lifetime: long enough that a form
!!! does not expire while it is being filled in, short enough that a shared
!!! machine does not stay logged in all afternoon.

public new-store returns session-store begin paragraph
    variable sessions as array of session is []
    give build session-store with sessions being sessions, lifetime being 3600000
end paragraph


public store-with-lifetime receives store as session-store, lifetime as number returns session-store begin paragraph
    give build session-store with sessions being store.sessions, lifetime being lifetime
end paragraph


!!! --- Begin a session under an id the caller supplies.
!!!
!!! `now` is passed in rather than read here, so a test can place a session in
!!! time without waiting for the clock and so one request's handling uses one
!!! consistent instant throughout.

public start-session receives store as session-store, id as text, now-at as number
    returns session-store begin paragraph
    variable sessions as array of session is []
    repeat for each existing in store.sessions
        when not (existing.id equals id) then
            append existing to sessions
        end when
    end repeat
    variable values-held as array of session-value is []
    append build session with id being id, values-held being values-held,
        expires-at being now-at plus store.lifetime to sessions
    give build session-store with sessions being sessions, lifetime being store.lifetime
end paragraph


!!! --- Whether a live session exists under this id.
!!! An expired session is not found, which is the same answer as never having
!!! existed — a client must not be able to tell the difference.

public has-session receives store as session-store, id as text, now-at as number returns boolean begin paragraph
    repeat for each existing in store.sessions
        when existing.id equals id and existing.expires-at is greater than now-at then
            give true
        end when
    end repeat
    give false
end paragraph


public value-in receives store as session-store, id as text, name as text, now-at as number
    returns text begin paragraph
    repeat for each existing in store.sessions
        when existing.id equals id and existing.expires-at is greater than now-at then
            repeat for each held in existing.values-held
                when held.name equals name then
                    give held.content
                end when
            end repeat
            give ""
        end when
    end repeat
    give ""
end paragraph


!!! --- Put a value in a session, and push its expiry out by the lifetime.
!!!
!!! Writing counts as activity. A session that only ever reads does not renew,
!!! which is a deliberate choice: a page left open in a background tab should
!!! not keep a session alive forever.

public set-in receives store as session-store, id as text, name as text, content as text, now-at as number
    returns session-store begin paragraph
    variable sessions as array of session is []
    repeat for each existing in store.sessions
        !!! Flat rather than nested: a branch holding its own `when` needs an
        !!! explicit arm block, and two conditions read better than one with
        !!! a tail. Expired sessions are dropped on the way past.
        value live is existing.expires-at is greater than now-at
        value target is existing.id equals id and live
        when target then
            variable values-held as array of session-value is []
            repeat for each held in existing.values-held
                when not (held.name equals name) then
                    append held to values-held
                end when
            end repeat
            append build session-value with name being name, content being content to values-held
            append build session with id being existing.id, values-held being values-held,
                expires-at being now-at plus store.lifetime to sessions
        end when
        when live and not target then
            append existing to sessions
        end when
    end repeat
    give build session-store with sessions being sessions, lifetime being store.lifetime
end paragraph


!!! --- End a session. Used on sign-out, and the half of the job that matters:
!!! the other half is overwriting the cookie, which `ended-cookie` does.

public end-session receives store as session-store, id as text returns session-store begin paragraph
    variable sessions as array of session is []
    repeat for each existing in store.sessions
        when not (existing.id equals id) then
            append existing to sessions
        end when
    end repeat
    give build session-store with sessions being sessions, lifetime being store.lifetime
end paragraph


!!! --- Drop everything that has expired. Nothing requires this to be called —
!!! an expired session is already invisible — but a long-running process that
!!! never calls it keeps every session it ever made.

public sweep-expired receives store as session-store, now-at as number returns session-store begin paragraph
    variable sessions as array of session is []
    repeat for each existing in store.sessions
        when existing.expires-at is greater than now-at then
            append existing to sessions
        end when
    end repeat
    give build session-store with sessions being sessions, lifetime being store.lifetime
end paragraph


public live-count receives store as session-store, now-at as number returns number begin paragraph
    variable total as number is 0
    repeat for each existing in store.sessions
        when existing.expires-at is greater than now-at then
            set total to total plus 1
        end when
    end repeat
    give total
end paragraph


public cookie-name returns text begin paragraph
    give "nilweb_session"
end paragraph


!!! --- The session id a request carries, or "" when it carries none.

public session-id-of receives request as http-request returns text begin paragraph
    give cookie-of with request being request, name being (cookie-name with nothing)
end paragraph


!!! --- Attach the session cookie to a response.
!!!
!!! The attributes are not configurable and that is the point. HttpOnly keeps
!!! the id out of document.cookie, so a cross-site script cannot read it.
!!! SameSite=Lax keeps it off cross-site POSTs, which is most of CSRF.
!!! Path=/ scopes it to the application. Max-Age matches the store's lifetime
!!! so the browser forgets it when the server does.
!!!
!!! `secure` is the one choice left to the caller, because a cookie marked
!!! Secure is not sent over plain HTTP and development is usually plain HTTP.
!!! Pass true everywhere a real user will ever reach.

public with-session-cookie receives response as http-response, id as text, store as session-store,
    secure as boolean returns http-response begin paragraph
    value seconds is store.lifetime over 1000
    variable attributes as text is "Path=/; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Max-Age=" plus (seconds becomes text)
    when secure then
        set attributes to attributes plus "; Secure"
    end when
    give with-cookie with response being response, name being (cookie-name with nothing),
        content being id, attributes being attributes
end paragraph


!!! --- Overwrite the cookie so the browser drops it, for sign-out.
!!! Max-Age=0 and an empty value, with the same attributes: a cookie is only
!!! replaced by one whose path and name match.

public with-ended-cookie receives response as http-response, secure as boolean returns http-response begin paragraph
    variable attributes as text is "Path=/; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Max-Age=0"
    when secure then
        set attributes to attributes plus "; Secure"
    end when
    give with-cookie with response being response, name being (cookie-name with nothing),
        content being "", attributes being attributes
end paragraph


!!! --- A session id for development and tests. NOT A CREDENTIAL.
!!!
!!! Derived from a counter and a caller-supplied number, so it is unique
!!! within one run and entirely predictable from outside it. Anyone who can
!!! guess the time your process started can guess every id it will ever issue.
!!! It is here so the demo runs, and it is named so that `development-id`
!!! appearing in a production diff is the review comment writing itself.

public variable issued as number is 0

public development-id receives now-at as number returns text begin paragraph
    set nilweb.session.session::issued to nilweb.session.session::issued plus 1
    give "dev-" plus (now-at becomes text) plus "-" plus (nilweb.session.session::issued becomes text)
end paragraph
